widget.solution:
#include <stdexcept>

class Position {
public:
    int qty() const { return qty_; }
    double avg_price() const { return avg_; }

    void buy(int qty, double px) {
        if (qty <= 0) throw std::invalid_argument("buy a positive quantity");
        avg_ = (qty_ * avg_ + qty * px) / (qty_ + qty);
        qty_ += qty;
    }

    void sell(int qty) {
        if (qty <= 0 || qty > qty_) throw std::invalid_argument("cannot go short");
        qty_ -= qty;
        if (qty_ == 0) avg_ = 0.0;
    }

private:
    int qty_ = 0;
    double avg_ = 0.0;
};

explain.text:
The constructor checks once. Every function that changes the data must check again, or it is a way round the rule.

```cpp
// before: skips the rule
void set_bid(double b) { bid_ = b; }
// after: checks, then stores
void set_bid(double b) {
    if (b >= ask_) throw std::invalid_argument("crossed");
    bid_ = b;
}
```

screens.prose:
Bid below ask, and never short: each is an **invariant**, a fact about the members that holds whenever outside code can look. A class keeps one in four places:

```cpp
class Quote {
public:
    Quote(double b, double a)       // 1. the constructor
        : bid_(b), ask_(a) {        //    builds, then checks
        if (bid_ >= ask_) throw std::invalid_argument("");
    }
    void set_bid(double b);         // 2. every change checks
    double mid() const;             // 3. readers are const
private:
    double bid_, ask_;              // 4. the data is private
};
```

